{"name":"Atom Blue","hex":"#8f9cac","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"atom","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The smallest possible amount of matter which still retains its identity as a chemical element, now known to consist of a nucleus surrounded by electrons. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A hypothetical particle posited by Greek philosophers as an ultimate and indivisible component of matter."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/atom"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Atom","blocks":[{"text":"Atoms are the basic particles of the chemical elements and the fundamental building blocks of matter. An atom consists of a nucleus of protons and generally neutrons, surrounded by an electromagnetically bound swarm of electrons. The chemical elements are distinguished from each other by the number of protons that are in their atoms. For example, any atom that contains 11 protons is sodium, and any atom that contains 29 protons is copper. Atoms with the same number of protons but a different number of neutrons are called isotopes of the same element."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Helium_atom_QM.svg/330px-Helium_atom_QM.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atom"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Daltons_symbols.gif/500px-Daltons_symbols.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Daltons_symbols.gif/500px-Daltons_symbols.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Various atoms and molecules from A New System of Chemical Philosophy (John Dalton, 1808)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADaltons_symbols.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Bohr_atom_animation_2.gif/250px-Bohr_atom_animation_2.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/17/Bohr_atom_animation_2.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Bohr_atom_animation_2.gif/250px-Bohr_atom_animation_2.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Bohr model of the atom, with an electron making instantaneous \"quantum leaps\" from one orbit to another with gain or loss of energy. This model of electrons in orbits is obsolete.","link":"htt